module nupp.tools.jitlimits

--[[
LuaJIT's capacity limits, raised for the whole process before anything is traced.

These are not the hotness thresholds `nupp.tools.cli` tunes per command. Those
decide which traces are worth compiling and depend on the program's shape. These
decide how much compiled code the VM may keep, and reaching one of them throws
all of it away: running out of trace numbers or of machine code flushes every
trace, and the program is interpreted again until it re-earns them. Nothing is
worth that, whatever the program is, so every command and every host applies
them.

Three limits did it, each measured on a cold `nupp check` of this repository:

* `sizemcode`. A trace must stay within branch range of the interpreter, which
  on arm64 is 128 MB. LuaJIT asks for each new machine-code area near the last
  one, but macOS ignores the placement hint and answers with the next free
  address, and once the heap has grown that is out of range. So the second area
  never comes, and each time the first fills -- 64 KB by default, a couple of
  hundred traces -- everything is flushed. One 16 MB area is reserved while the
  process is small enough for it to land in range.
* `maxtrace`. The default of 1000 live traces flushed the main compiler process
  ninety-one times on its own.
* `maxside`. A root trace takes at most 100 side traces, and a loop reached with
  more shapes than that falls back to the interpreter for the rest: a shared
  loop called with 150 closure prototypes ran 22x slower than with 90. At 1000
  it compiles them all.

Together they took the cold check from 2,737 flushes and 251,008 compiled traces
to none and about 14,600, and from 21-25 s to 18.3-18.8 s. `bench/jit-limits.lua`
repeats the measurement. A larger area buys nothing more (32 MB measured the
same), and LuaJIT reprotects the whole area around each trace, so a much larger
one costs compile time.
]]

local jitlimits = {}

--- The limits, each as its own `jit.opt.start` argument.
---
--- The native host applies the same list to every state it creates, in
--- `native/crates/host/c/lua_shim.c`; `clitest` holds the two to one account.
jitlimits.FLAGS = {"maxtrace=20000", "maxside=1000", "sizemcode=16384", "maxmcode=16384"}

--- Raises the limits, unless `NUPP_JIT_DEFAULT` asks for LuaJIT's own.
---
--- `NUPP_JIT_LIMITS` replaces them with a comma-separated flag list, empty for
--- none, which is how `bench/jit-limits.lua` compares settings without rebuilding
--- the compiler between them.
---
--- An area already allocated keeps the size it was made with, so whatever was
--- traced before this is flushed and the next trace allocates at the new size.
--- Called first thing, that is next to nothing.
---
--- @return whether any limit was applied
function jitlimits.apply(): boolean
    if os.getenv("NUPP_JIT_DEFAULT") then
        return false
    end
    local flags = jitlimits.FLAGS
    local override = os.getenv("NUPP_JIT_LIMITS")
    if override then
        flags = {}
        for flag in override:gmatch("[^,]+") do
            flags[#flags + 1] = flag
        end
    end
    if #flags == 0 then
        return false
    end
    -- A LuaJIT built without the JIT, or a future one that renames a flag, is not a
    -- reason to refuse to run.
    if not pcall(jit.opt.start, unpack(flags)) then
        return false
    end
    jit.flush()

    return true
end

export = jitlimits
